              "text": "A college library wanted to give students better (1) to practical information about the surrounding town. Its website listed transport services, but the details were scattered across several pages. Staff decided to (2) the information in one guide that students could consult before leaving campus.\n\nThe project began with a walk around the neighborhood. Volunteers checked bus stops and photographed notices, including a (3) sign whose missing letters made the destination impossible to read. They did not simply copy everything they found. Instead, they compared the notices with information from the service offices. This helped (4) the risk of including details that were no longer correct.\n\nThe volunteers learned to record dates (5), checking every number against the original notice before saving a photograph. They also discovered a surprising (6) of needs: some students wanted bus routes, while others needed places to repair bicycles or buy inexpensive food. A (7), rather than lengthy, introduction of just two sentences explained how to use the guide.\n\nThe guide did not become popular overnight. Its readership grew (8), a little each week, as students recommended it to friends. Questions from readers provided (9) that several pages needed clearer headings. Updating the guide soon became a (10) task, carried out every Friday as part of the library's regular work.",
