              "look": "passage",
              "text": "A student team studied how a campus cafe could waste less food without offering fewer choices. The manager gave them (1) to observe the kitchen, but asked them not to interrupt staff during the busiest hours. The students began by making a (2) record of the meals left unsold at the end of each day.\n\nTheir first suggestion was to (3) smaller portions of side dishes, with extra servings available on request. This would allow customers to choose an amount that matched their appetite. The team also asked staff to (4) unused ingredients from food that had already been served. Combining the two would make the records misleading.\n\nThe figures showed that soup was often left over, whereas sandwiches usually sold out. Yet the team avoided a (5) judgment based on a single day. They watched for two weeks and checked whether unusual events had affected demand. When a sports meeting brought extra visitors, they marked that day (6) so it would not be mistaken for an ordinary afternoon.\n\nChanges were introduced (7), with a different adjustment tested each week rather than all at once. Staff expressed (8) about the additional work, so the students designed a form that took only a minute to complete. The resulting system was (9) rather than complicated, making it suitable for daily use. Its main (10) was a clearer picture of what customers actually ate, allowing the manager to plan purchases with less guesswork.",
